    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "but it doesn't feel like a 'journey' or 'growth'. it just feels like i'm haunting my own life. everything we did, everything we planned, we were one unit. now i'm just half a person trying to force myself to function, but there's nothing left inside to drive it. it's so exhausting pretending to be a person when the person i actually was died with him.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I'm deeply sorry that you're feeling this way. Grief can sometimes create a void that feels overwhelming, especially when you're used to a life where significant parts of you and your loved one were intertwined. It's a testament to the depth of your connection, but that doesn't have to be a defining or constraining part of your future. First and foremost, please don't give up. Your feelings are valid, and it's important to allow yourself to feel them fully, even when it feels consuming. One step at a time, you might find small moments that bring you comfort or joy, even amidst the pain. Seeking support from a grief counselor or joining a support group can also help you feel less alone in navigating these challenges. It's an uneven road, but it's possible to rediscover and build on what makes you whole again, even without your loved one.",
